From 675bc8c8b39cfc8610d07e0ea53b300a4f889332 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wendell Britt <wendell@masteringallyship.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 23:06:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Move the list onto Resend Audiences, and unwire Kit

KIT_API_KEY was never set in production. Every lead logged "[kit] not
configured -- skipped sync" while the Resend send in the same request
went through, so the site had two email vendors and only one of them
working. Resend now covers what Kit was chosen for: contacts, contact
properties, segments, broadcasts and event-triggered automations, all
present in the resend@6.14.0 SDK already installed here.

Adds a Resend Audiences client with the same exported name and the same
input shape, so the six call sites move by one import path each. The
mapping: a Kit tag becomes a Resend segment resolved by name and created
if absent, a Kit custom field becomes a contact property, a Kit
subscriber becomes a contact. Same persist-then-send contract, same
never-throws guarantee, same graceful skip when unconfigured, same
name-resolution cache.

list-contract.ts does not move at all. It is pure and decides policy
before any network call, so the promise it encodes -- roughly four
broadcasts a year to the Kickstarter backers and no funnel -- survived
the provider swap untouched. That was the point of writing it that way.

kit.ts is kept rather than deleted, with a header saying it is unwired
and how to put it back. Nothing imports it.

One behavioural difference worth knowing: Kit created custom fields
implicitly, Resend rejects a property value whose key was never
declared, so the keys are ensured before the contact write and cached.

tsc --noEmit and eslint both clean. Smoke-tested the two degradation
paths: unconfigured returns skipped, empty email returns an error, and
neither throws.
